/**
 * @page corruption HX711 corruption on STUAART V2 (GPIO 9 / flash bus)
 *
 * @section corr_summary Summary
 *
 * On the STUAART V2 PCB, cell 1 DOUT is routed to FireBeetle pin **D5**,
 * which maps to **ESP32 GPIO 9**. On the ESP32-D0WD die used by this
 * FireBeetle revision, GPIO 9 is bonded internally to the external SPI
 * flash bus. The flash controller drives that pad on every CPU cache
 * miss, in parallel with the HX711 driving its DOUT to the same node.
 * The two drivers fight, and a fraction of cell 1 reads come out
 * corrupted.
 *
 * Statistical analysis of the production CSV from 2026-03-10
 * (4.25 M rows over 13 hours) shows :
 *
 * | Cell | DOUT pin | Outliers | Dominant value | % corruption |
 * | ---  | ---      | ---      | ---            | ---          |
 * | 1    | GPIO 9   | 2868     | -160.68 g      | ~0.066 %     |
 * | 2    | GPIO 27  | 450      | -78.45 g       | ~0.011 %     |
 * | 3    | GPIO 16  | < 50     | scattered      | < 0.001 %    |
 *
 * The −160.68 g spike on cell 1 maps exactly to a raw HX711 reading of
 * `0xFFFFFF` (= `-1L` after sign extension), the signature of a DOUT
 * line held HIGH throughout the 24-bit shift. ~98 % of cell 1 outliers
 * are this exact value.
 *
 * @section corr_pad The shared pad : two drivers on one wire
 *
 * Each pad on the ESP32-D0WD is a multiplexer between several internal
 * functions and the external pin. For GPIO 6 to 11, one of those
 * functions is the dedicated flash SPI controller, hardwired to the
 * pad. `pinMode(9, INPUT)` from the user code only affects the GPIO
 * matrix function ; it does NOT disconnect the flash function, which
 * is implemented in fixed silicon logic.
 *
 * @dot
 * digraph pad_share {
 *   graph [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, rankdir=LR];
 *   node  [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, style=rounded];
 *
 *   subgraph cluster_die {
 *     label = "ESP32-D0WD die";
 *     style = dashed;
 *     gpio   [label="GPIO matrix\n(pinMode INPUT)", shape=box];
 *     flash  [label="Flash SPI\ncontroller\n(hardware path)", shape=box, color=red, fontcolor=red];
 *   }
 *
 *   pad    [label="GPIO 9\n(physical pad)", shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fillcolor=lightyellow];
 *   chip   [label="External\nflash chip", shape=cylinder];
 *   hx     [label="HX711 cell 1\nDOUT", shape=box];
 *
 *   gpio  -> pad [label="reads only\n(weak)"];
 *   flash -> pad [label="drives\n(strong)", color=red, fontcolor=red];
 *   pad   -> chip [label="SD2 / WP", color=red, fontcolor=red];
 *   hx    -> pad [label="drives\n(strong)", dir=forward];
 * }
 * @enddot
 *
 * The flash bus toggles at multi-MHz whenever the CPU executes from
 * flash (which is essentially always). When the user code does
 * `digitalRead(9)` to clock in a HX711 bit, the pad voltage at that
 * instant is whichever driver is winning. If the flash signal is HIGH
 * for the full duration of the shift (which happens during a multi-byte
 * burst), all 24 bits read back as 1 → `0xFFFFFF`.
 *
 * @section corr_signatures The three known signatures
 *
 * The library recognises three values that cannot occur during normal
 * operation of a strain-gauge load cell :
 *
 * | Signature  | Meaning                              | Cause                                   |
 * | ---        | ---                                  | ---                                     |
 * | `0xFFFFFF` (`-1L`)         | DOUT held HIGH throughout the read | Interrupt during SCK pulse > 60 us, OR flash bus contention on GPIO 6-11 |
 * | `0x800000` (`-8388608L`)   | 24-bit negative saturation         | Boot tare hits a fully-corrupted read   |
 * | `0x7FFFFF` (`+8388607L`)   | 24-bit positive saturation         | Mirror image of negative saturation     |
 *
 * @section corr_safe_read safe_read() : retry on corruption
 *
 * @ref LoadCell::safe_read calls `read()` and, if the result matches
 * one of the three signatures, retries up to three times. Each retry
 * costs exactly one HX711 conversion (12.5 ms at 80 Hz), so the
 * worst-case overhead per cell is ~37 ms. In practice cells 2 and 3
 * almost never trigger retries, and cell 1 retries succeed on the
 * second attempt about half the time.
 *
 * @dot
 * digraph safe_read_flow {
 *   graph [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, rankdir=TB];
 *   node  [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, shape=box, style=rounded];
 *
 *   start  [label="caller", shape=ellipse];
 *   read1  [label="raw = read()"];
 *   chk1   [label="hx711_corrupted(raw) ?", shape=diamond];
 *   retry  [label="raw = read()\n(up to 3 times)"];
 *   chk2   [label="still corrupted ?", shape=diamond];
 *   ret    [label="return raw\n(may be -1L)", shape=ellipse];
 *
 *   start -> read1 -> chk1;
 *   chk1  -> ret    [label="no"];
 *   chk1  -> retry  [label="yes"];
 *   retry -> chk2;
 *   chk2  -> retry  [label="yes,\nattempts left"];
 *   chk2  -> ret    [label="no, or budget spent"];
 * }
 * @enddot
 *
 * The averaging methods (@ref LoadCell::read_raw_average,
 * @ref LoadCell::read_tare_average, @ref LoadCell::read_scale_coeff_average)
 * call `safe_read()` and then exclude any sample that still looks
 * corrupted from the mean. The divisor is the count of GOOD samples,
 * not the requested `n_readings`, so a single bad read does not pull
 * the average toward `0xFFFFFF`.
 *
 * @section corr_limits Limits of the software fix
 *
 * The three signatures cover ~98 % of cell 1 corruption observed in
 * production. The remaining 2 % are intermediate bit-patterns that
 * happen when the flash bus is partway through a transition during
 * the 24-bit shift : the read returns a plausible-looking value
 * (e.g. `-2 477 564`) that is wrong but indistinguishable from a
 * legitimate reading.
 *
 * No software primitive can detect these residual cases without
 * external context (e.g. statistical comparison with neighbouring
 * cells, or a median over a sliding window). A median filter over
 * `weight_n_readings = 5` would catch most of them at the cost of
 * dropping the effective sample rate to ~17 Hz — see the discussion
 * in `Firmware/docs/improvements-to-port.md`.
 *
 * @section corr_hw_fix The hardware fix
 *
 * The proper resolution is to reroute cell 1 DOUT off GPIO 9. Two
 * pins are unconnected on the V2 PCB and ideal for a strap :
 *
 * - **D2 = GPIO 25**
 * - **D3 = GPIO 26**
 *
 * Recommended procedure :
 *
 * 1. Remove the FireBeetle from its socket on the PCB.
 * 2. Bend the D5 leg outward so it cannot re-enter the socket. This
 *    leaves the original PCB trace U1-DAT → D5 dangling but
 *    electrically disconnected.
 * 3. Solder a thin wire from the U1-DAT pad (DOUT pin of HX711 #1) to
 *    the D2 or D3 socket pin (or directly to the matching FireBeetle
 *    pad if more convenient).
 * 4. Re-seat the FireBeetle.
 * 5. In `STUAART.ino`, change `controller.add_loadcell(loadCell1, 9, 17)`
 *    to `controller.add_loadcell(loadCell1, 25, 17)` (or `26, 17`).
 *
 * The strap is reversible (just bend the leg back and remove the wire)
 * and avoids cutting any PCB trace. After rework, cell 1 corruption
 * drops to the same baseline as cells 2 and 3 (~0.01 %, dominated by
 * residual interrupt-induced cases that `safe_read` continues to
 * filter).
 *
 * @section corr_diag Diagnosing corruption in CSV files
 *
 * The CSV format includes the raw 24-bit signed counts as the last
 * three columns. To make the corruption signatures jump out, run the
 * post-processing script :
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * python3 PostProcessingCode/add_binary_columns.py today.csv > today_with_bin.csv
 * @endcode
 *
 * It appends three columns that show each raw value as its 24-bit
 * two's-complement bit pattern. A row with `111111111111111111111111`
 * in the cell 1 binary column is a `0xFFFFFF` corruption signature
 * that escaped `safe_read` (which should not happen post-strap).
 *
 * @section corr_stats Live corruption statistics
 *
 * Each `LoadCell` instance keeps four lifetime counters incremented
 * inside @ref LoadCell::safe_read :
 *
 * - `total_reads`      : every HX711 conversion consumed
 * - `corrupted_neg1`   : count of `0xFFFFFF` (-1L) signatures
 * - `corrupted_negsat` : count of `0x800000` (-8388608L) signatures
 * - `corrupted_possat` : count of `0x7FFFFF` (+8388607L) signatures
 *
 * The counters survive across `safe_read` calls but reset on a
 * power-cycle / soft reboot, or whenever the user issues a
 * `reset stats` serial command.
 *
 * To inspect them at runtime, type `stats` on the serial port :
 *
 * @code
 * > stats
 * --- HX711 read statistics ---
 * cell 1 : reads=85234, 0xFFFFFF=56, 0x800000=0, 0x7FFFFF=0, total_bad=56 (0.0657 %)
 * cell 2 : reads=85234, 0xFFFFFF=9,  0x800000=0, 0x7FFFFF=0, total_bad=9  (0.0106 %)
 * cell 3 : reads=85234, 0xFFFFFF=0,  0x800000=0, 0x7FFFFF=0, total_bad=0  (0.0000 %)
 * @endcode
 *
 * Use case : compare cells before and after the GPIO 9 strap. Pre-strap
 * cell 1 will show ~0.05-0.07 % corruption (essentially the production
 * baseline) ; post-strap it should drop to the same low level as cells
 * 2 and 3 (< 0.02 %, dominated by residual interrupt-induced cases that
 * `safe_read` continues to filter).
 */
